{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>You shall surely give him.</b> The force of the duplication is to say: if you give, the Holy One, blessed be He, will add to your wealth, and you will return and give again. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: נתון תתן — even a hundred times. The sense is that it does not suffice for him if he gives once only, but he is obligated to give him several times, and concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 21): ״הצדיק יתן ולא יחשוך״ — that is, and does not withhold from giving. Furthermore, נתון תתן may also include: give him with a pleasant disposition and in a way of sincerity, not by way of rebuke and arrogance of spirit, for thereby his wealth endures. Concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 13): ״כפר נפש איש עשרו ורש לא שמע גערה״ — when does a man's wealth endure and serve him as ransom and atonement? When the poor man hears no rebuke from the rich man, but rather he gives him with a good heart and a cheerful countenance. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Rabbi Yannai saw a certain man giving charity to a poor man in public. He said to him: 'It would have been better had you not given to him than that you gave to him and shamed him.'" "<b>Because on account of this thing.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: it did not say 'on account of' (בעבור) and did not say 'for the sake of' (למען) but rather בגלל — it is an expression of גלגל (wheel), that is to say, it is a wheel that turns in the world: if you do not give to him, I will turn the wheel and make you poor and him rich, as it is said (Psalms 75): ״כי אלהים שופט זה ישפיל וזה ירים״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: a person should always seek mercy concerning this attribute, for if it does not come upon him it comes upon his son, and if not upon his son, upon his son's son." "<b>May YHWH your Elohim bless you.</b> He fixed blessing upon the commandment of charity, and that is what Solomon said (Proverbs 11): ״יש מפזר ונוסף עוד״. And now I shall rouse you to the supreme greatness of this esteemed commandment which we inherited from our father Abraham, and to establish you upon its foundation and its secret. Know that the poor man partakes of the attribute of judgment, for the attribute of judgment is stretched out against him; this is (Psalms 102): ״תפלה לעני״ — for תפלה is from the language of judgment and pleading. Therefore, among the masters of truth, the poor man is accounted as dead, while the rich man who has blessing with him partakes of the attribute of mercy. It is like the simile of the sun that illuminates the moon, and the moon receives its light from the sun; so too the giver of charity illuminates the eyes of the poor man and receives his blessing from him. Therefore the Torah cautions — all whose ways are ways of pleasantness and all whose paths are peace — that a person should habituate himself in the trait of compassion, giving from his blessing as charity to the poor, and in doing so he joins the attribute of mercy with the attribute of judgment. That joining is the sustenance of the world, and it cannot endure without both of them. And you already know that charity is drawn toward צדק (Righteousness), and there it pours forth its influence. And behold, Jerusalem is called (Isaiah 1): ״עיר הצדק״, and it is written (ibid.): ״צדק ילין בה״, and the Prophet said to them (ibid. 54): ״בצדקה תכונני״. For צדק is Knesset Israel, the partner of the Sabbath. If so, he cautioned concerning the commandment of charity, and one who gives charity to the poor below — behold, he draws down charity above, to צדק. Concerning this the masters of truth said in the pearls of their sayings: 'Great is charity for its own sake' — they intended to say that from there blessing flows forth to the lowly, and this is what King David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119): ״ואשא כפי אל מצותיך אשר אהבתי״." "<b>And further,</b> you will find in Midrash Tehillim, and it is a hint to this: see how great is the power of charity, that through the small coin that a person gives to a poor man he merits and receives the face of the Shekhinah. In the way of the world, a noblewoman who seeks to be received in audience before the king fashions a crown befitting his honor, and by means of the crown with which she comes to crown the king, she sees the face of the king. So too, by means of charity a person receives the face of the Shekhinah, as it is said (Psalms 17): ״אני בצדק אחזה פניך אשבעה בהקיץ תמונתך״. And the fact that the passage began with ״באחד שעריך בארצך״ and likewise concluded with ״בארצך״ indicates that the essence of charity is only in the Land, even though it is an obligation of the body in every place. And the same applies to all the other commandments, whose essential obligation is in the Land, for thus he mentioned at the beginning of the parashah: ״אלה החקים והמשפטים אשר תשמרון לעשות בארץ״ — because all the commandments are the law of the divine force of the Land. Our Rabbis of blessed memory already mentioned that when we perform them outside the Land it is so that we not forget them when we return to the Land, and this is what they said, interpreting (Deuteronomy 11): ״ושמתם את דברי אלה על לבבכם״ — even after you are exiled, be distinguished with commandments: put on tefillin, make a mezuzah, so that they not be new to you when you return to the Land; and so it says (Jeremiah 31): ״הציבי לך ציונים״. Alternatively, the meaning of the word בארצך may be: even in your land — and this obligates the commandment of charity outside the Land, for it is an obligation of the body in every place, and it is an intelligible commandment." "<b>And the reason</b> of the passage: guard yourself lest you say, 'The seventh year, the year of shemittah, is near, and if I must remit my money or my lands in the Land, how shall I give charity? It turns out that I lose from every side.' Therefore it says: ״ולא ירע לבבך בתתך לו כי בגלל הדבר הזה״ etc. Therefore I command you, saying, that you shall open your hand to your poor and to your needy — even in your land, where you remit what is yours, whether the remission of lands or the remission of money — there I caution you concerning charity, for the Blessed Name will bless you on this account and add to what is yours. All the more so must you give charity outside the Land, where there is no remission of lands. And we learn from this that when it mentions בארצך, it did not come to diminish outside the Land; for had the intent been 'in the Land' exclusively, Scripture would have mentioned ״כי תבאו אל הארץ״ or ״בבאכם אל הארץ״, in the manner we find with commandments dependent on the Land."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:44.476647324Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}